    "body": "*Structural and sourcing guidance in this guide is generic industry knowledge. Terms marked as ELAPACK figures are the factory's confirmed 2026 trade terms; everything else should be confirmed with the supplier you are evaluating.*\n\nThis guide is for founders and packaging buyers planning or running a subscription box programme \u2014 beauty, candle, jewellery, snack or gift clubs \u2014 who need to buy subscription box packaging that works in month ten as well as month one. Companion pages cover [what a box costs](https://elapack.com/news/subscription-box-packaging-cost/) and when to order it; this page covers the decisions that come first. If your question is theme design or vendor selection, stop here: this page does not design editions or pick a supplier. **A subscription box bought like a one-off box is a monthly mistake; the box has to be specified for the programme \u2014 the repeat, the variation and the transit \u2014 not for one edition.** Work through the five decisions below and you will finish with a programme spec you can hand to any supplier.\n\n## Why subscription boxes get bought wrong\n\nA subscription box looks like a box, so it gets specified like one: one structure, one quote, one order. Then the programme starts, and the box meets the three things a one-off never has to survive \u2014 repetition, variation and a monthly calendar.\n\n1. A founder specifies a beautiful box for launch month and orders it like any custom box.\n2. Month two needs a new theme; month three needs a taller product; month five needs 3,000 units, not 500.\n3. **Each month re-opens decisions the spec never fixed \u2014 because the box was specified for an edition, not for the programme.**\n4. The programme pays for it as repeated sampling, re-tooling and air freight, or as a box that quietly stops changing with the theme; [planning split shipments](https://elapack.com/news/plan-split-packaging-shipments/) is one structural alternative.\n5. By winter the [unboxing](https://elapack.com/news/unboxing-experience-guide/) looks like everyone else's, because variation was never designed in.\n6. The task on this page is to make the five programme-level decisions up front, so monthly editions become print changes on a settled structure.\n\nThe fix is not a better launch box. It is a specification that assumes the box will repeat and change at the same time.\n\n## Five decisions that specify a subscription box programme\n\n![Five decisions that specify a subscription box programme, with replenishment cadence as the deciding constraint](/charts/subscription-box-programme-spec.svg)\n\n**Decide the programme before the edition: structure, insides, variation, transit, terms.** Each decision ends with the question your programme has to answer.\n\n### Decision 1 \u2014 The box structure, chosen for repeat opening\n\nCompare a folding carton, corrugated mailer or presentation rigid box according to product fit, packing workflow, storage and delivery route. Magnetic closure is an option within a presentation-box design, not proof of parcel protection. If reuse matters, agree opening-cycle and appearance checks on the loaded sample; do not infer durability from board weight.\n\n### Decision 2 \u2014 What goes inside: pouch, insert, card\n\nSpecify each inside component by its task: restraint, surface separation, presentation or care information. Request the proposed fabric composition and weight, insert material and card dimensions in the quote. Generic material bands are not confirmed ELAPACK specifications.\n\n### Decision 3 \u2014 Variation mechanics: what changes monthly\n\nList what may change between editions: contents, artwork, sleeve, insert, ribbon or structure. Ask which changes require a revised drawing, proof, sample or setup charge. A sleeve-based design is one option to compare, not a guarantee that all future editions can reuse the same box or cost less.\n\n### Decision 4 \u2014 Transit survival, every month\n\nAssess the complete pack for parcel handling. A presentation box may need an outer shipping carton. Agree route-appropriate checks using the actual contents, including restraint and relevant temperature conditions. Confirm the acceptance criteria before the recurring programme begins.\n\n### Decision 5 \u2014 Supplier terms that fit a recurring calendar\n\nELAPACK\u2019s confirmed minimum is 200 pieces; a complete set requires 200 pieces for each component. This does not automatically allow 200 pieces per colour, artwork or theme variant: confirm the proposed split in the quotation. Typical production is 15\u201320 days after sample approval and payment receipt, subject to order confirmation. Custom samples are USD 25 plus USD 20 shipping, with a 3\u20135 day sample-making period. Confirm repeat-order pricing, approval steps and capacity for the intended calendar.\n\nRead the five together. A box that passes structure and fails variation, or passes price and fails transit, is still wrong for a programme.\n\n## When a good-looking box is still a wrong programme buy\n\n**Evaluate the launch presentation and the recurring packing programme separately.** The first sample does not establish the fit, schedule or delivered cost of later editions.\n\n- Check each proposed edition against the approved structure and identify changes that require review.\n- Separate recurring and non-recurring charges where applicable; not every design requires the same tooling or print setup.\n- Agree route-appropriate transport checks before dispatch; a second edition alone does not prove programme-wide performance.\n\n## The programme spec list\n\nNothing on this page collects your data or sends anything. **Fill the programme spec before you brief a supplier, so the programme is bought once.** Copy the lines below into your own note:\n\n1. Programme: what ships monthly, typical unit count per edition, and how many variants per theme.\n2. Box: structure (folding carton, tuck mailer, magnetic flip-top, rigid lid), finished size in cm, and whether subscribers keep it.\n3. Insides: pouch fabric and band, insert material, card format \u2014 each with its job named.\n4. Variation: what changes monthly (artwork only? sleeve? ribbon colour?) and which charges are one-time.\n5. Transit: the product's failure mode in a parcel, and the test that proves the box handles it.\n6. Terms: MOQ, lead time bands, sample terms \u2014 confirmed in writing for a recurring calendar.\n7. Calendar: the monthly order date that keeps 15\u201320 day production ahead of each pack week.\n\nKeep the note local, and send it to your supplier through your existing approved contact process \u2014 do not send it anywhere this page has not verified. With the programme spec filled, the companion pages take over: the cost breakdown builds the budget layer by layer, and the timeline guide turns the calendar into dates. A subscription programme that specifies once and varies cheaply is the whole game \u2014 the box is just where it shows."
